## The in-session micro-loop

Inside the productive band, a smaller loop runs repeatedly. This is the practical operationalisation of [[Part 2.1 - Deliberate Practice|deliberate practice]] inside a focused block:

> [!check] The micro-loop (runs every 5–15 minutes inside the block)
> 1. **Visual anchorage.** Fix your eyes on the specific thing you're about to engage with (the page, the line of code, the problem). Narrow attention before broadening engagement.
> 2. **Generate an error signal early.** Predict, attempt, draft — *before* consuming. (The mechanisms from [[Part 2.1 - Deliberate Practice|Part 2.1]] live here.)
> 3. **Gap effect** — when transitioning between micro-tasks, give yourself 10–20 seconds of doing nothing. Just sit. The brain consolidates the previous micro-task in those seconds; rushing into the next one wipes it.
> 4. **Broaden visual field on exit.** Look up, look around, briefly. Signals to the nervous system that a sub-section closed.

A focused block contains perhaps 6–12 of these micro-loops. Each one is a small focus → small rest → small consolidation cycle, nested inside the larger focus → rest → consolidation cycle of the block itself. The fractal is intentional.

